{"ghsa_id":"GHSA-25q3-v2hm-8vpf","cve_id":"CVE-2026-93592","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/security-advisories/GHSA-25q3-v2hm-8vpf","html_url":"https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/security/advisories/GHSA-25q3-v2hm-8vpf","summary":"Denial of service: a negative token id in `/v1/embeddings` or `/pooling` input kills the vLLM engine via a CUDA device-side assertion","description":"### Summary\r\n\r\nA single request carrying a **negative token id** in the token-id form of the `input` field of `/v1/embeddings` or `/pooling` terminates the vLLM engine. The request is answered HTTP 500 and the server then serves nothing further: `/health` refuses the connection and every subsequent request from every client fails until the process is restarted.\r\n\r\nNo authentication, no prior request, and no unusual value are required. `-1` is enough.\r\n\r\n\r\n### Details\r\n\r\n`/v1/embeddings` and `/pooling` accept token ids directly from the caller (`input: list[int] | list[list[int]] | str | list[str]` on `CompletionRequestMixin`, in `vllm/entrypoints/pooling/base/protocol.py`). The engine validated only the **upper** bound of those ids against the vocabulary, in `InputProcessor._validate_model_input`:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nif max_input_id > max(tokenizer.max_token_id, model_vocab_size - 1):\r\n    raise ...   # \"Token id ... is out of vocabulary\"  -> HTTP 400\r\n```\r\n\r\nThere was no corresponding lower-bound check, so a negative id passed validation and was used directly as an index into the embedding table on the GPU. That trips a device-side assertion inside PyTorch's index kernel:\r\n\r\n```\r\naten/src/ATen/native/cuda/Indexing.cu:1553: indexSelectSmallIndex:\r\n    block: [4,0,0], thread: [32,0,0]\r\n    Assertion `srcIndex < srcSelectDimSize` failed.\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis is **not** a catchable Python `IndexError`. A CUDA device-side assertion poisons the CUDA context: after it fires, every subsequent CUDA operation in the process fails, including operations belonging to unrelated requests. That is why the process cannot recover and why `/health` refuses the connection rather than answering 503. A `try/except` around the forward pass cannot turn this into a 4xx — the ids have to be bounded **before** they reach the device.\r\n\r\nThis is not a PyTorch defect. The assertion is PyTorch's protection working as designed; without it an out-of-range id would read unmapped memory. PyTorch checks index *values* host-side only on CPU, because doing so on CUDA would require a synchronization on every indexing op (pytorch/pytorch#71572). The resulting contract is that callers must validate indices before handing them over, and vLLM was not doing so on this path.\r\n\r\n### Proof of concept\r\n\r\nAgainst a freshly started server (the first request is a control showing that the token-id input form is legal for this endpoint):\r\n\r\n```bash\r\nM=Qwen/Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B\r\nvllm serve $M --port 8000 --max-model-len 2048 --enforce-eager &\r\n\r\n# 1. control -- valid token ids\r\ncurl -sS -X POST http://localhost:8000/v1/embeddings \\\r\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\r\n  -d \"{\\\"model\\\":\\\"$M\\\",\\\"input\\\":[[9707,1879]]}\"\r\n# -> 200, /health -> 200\r\n\r\n# 2. the same request with one id made negative\r\ncurl -sS -X POST http://localhost:8000/v1/embeddings \\\r\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\r\n  -d \"{\\\"model\\\":\\\"$M\\\",\\\"input\\\":[[-1,1879]]}\"\r\n# -> 500 {\"error\":{\"message\":\"EngineCore encountered an issue. See stack trace\r\n#          (above) for the root cause.\",\"type\":\"InternalServerError\",\"code\":500}}\r\n# then /health refuses the connection and the server is gone\r\n```\r\n\r\n`/pooling` behaves identically. Either list position triggers it, and `-1` behaves the same as `INT64_MIN`, so this is any negative id rather than a boundary value.\r\n\r\n### Impact\r\n\r\nAny deployment that serves a pooling or embedding model through the OpenAI-compatible API server. One unauthenticated HTTP request is a complete denial of service for that server process and every client using it. Restarting under a supervisor does not mitigate it, because the same request can be sent again immediately.\r\n\r\nConfidentiality and integrity are unaffected; the impact is availability only.\r\n\r\n### Verification\r\n\r\nMeasured on stock upstream release images (`vllm/vllm-openai`, aarch64), one request per freshly started server, with a valid control request immediately before the negative one:\r\n\r\n| build | endpoint | control | negative id `-1` | `/health` after | valid request after | device-side asserts in log |\r\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\r\n| **v0.27.1** (unpatched) | `/v1/embeddings` | 200 | **500** | **connection refused** | **connection refused** | 3 |\r\n| **v0.27.1** (unpatched) | `/pooling` | 200 | **500** | **connection refused** | **connection refused** | 3 |\r\n| **v0.28.0** (patched) | `/v1/embeddings` | 200 | **400** `Token id -1 is out of vocabulary` | 200 | 200 | 0 |\r\n| **v0.28.0** (patched) | `/pooling` | 200 | **400** `Token id -1 is out of vocabulary` | 200 | 200 | 0 |\r\n\r\n`INT64_MIN` (`-9223372036854775808`) behaves identically to `-1` on both builds, and either position in the id list triggers it, so this is any negative id rather than a boundary value.\r\n\r\nOn v0.27.1 the engine process reports:\r\n\r\n```\r\ntorch.AcceleratorError: CUDA error: device-side assert triggered\r\n```\r\n\r\nwith the underlying kernel assertion being\r\n\r\n```\r\naten/src/ATen/native/cuda/Indexing.cu:1553: indexSelectSmallIndex:\r\n    Assertion `srcIndex < srcSelectDimSize` failed.\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Patches\r\n\r\nFixed in **0.28.0** by https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/51795 (commit `b216db3ed0`), which adds the missing lower-bound check to `InputProcessor._validate_model_input` so a negative id is rejected as HTTP 400 before the ids reach the device:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nif min_input_id < 0:\r\n    raise VLLMValidationError(f\"Token id {min_input_id} is out of vocabulary\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe validation path is shared by generate, embedding and pooling requests, so the check covers all three.\r\n\r\n### Workarounds\r\n\r\nFor deployments that cannot upgrade to 0.28.0:\r\n\r\n- Reject request bodies containing negative integers in `input` at a reverse proxy or API gateway in front of vLLM.\r\n- Do not expose the token-id form of the input; accept only string input from untrusted callers.\r\n- Restrict network access to `/v1/embeddings` and `/pooling`.\r\n\r\nProcess supervision is **not** a workaround: the request can simply be repeated.\r\n\r\n### Not affected\r\n\r\n- `/v1/completions` — its `prompt` field constrains token ids with `Field(ge=0)`, so pydantic rejects a negative id before the engine sees it. That constraint has been present since v0.17.0, and was confirmed on an unpatched v0.27.1 build: `CompletionRequest(model=..., prompt=[[-1, 5]])` raises `ValidationError`.\r\n- `/score` and `/rerank` — these do not accept a token-id input form at all.\r\n\r\n### References\r\n\r\n- https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/51795\r\n- https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/issues/71572","severity":"high","author":null,"publisher":{"login":"jperezdealgaba","id":124347725,"node_id":"U_kgDOB2llTQ","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/124347725?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jperezdealgaba","html_url":"https://github.com/jperezdealgaba","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jperezdealgaba/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jperezdealgaba/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jperezdealgaba/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jperezdealgaba/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jperezdealgaba/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jperezdealgaba/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jperezd